# Python style guide

Follow the configured Ruff rules in `pyproject.toml`. This document specifies
the project conventions that complement those rules.

## Code and documentation

- Use double quotes for all strings.
- Add type hints to all code.
- Use NumPy-style docstrings only.
- Thoroughly document public classes and methods.
- Private classes, methods, globals, and similar implementation details may
  have brief documentation or none when their purpose is clear from context.
- Make errors and warnings concise and informative. Avoid multi-sentence
  messages unless a single sentence would sacrifice clarity.

## Annotations and imports

{% if minimum_python.split('.')[1] | int >= 15 %}Annotations are deferred (PEP 649) and must evaluate at runtime: libraries
such as Pydantic, dispatch frameworks, and `inspect.signature` introspect them.

- Do not use `from __future__ import annotations`, `if TYPE_CHECKING:` import
  blocks, or quoted annotations.
- Import names used only in annotations, or whose eager import would create an
  import cycle, with module-level `lazy import` or `lazy from ... import ...`
  (PEP 810).
- Keep `TYPE_CHECKING` only for deliberate type-checker-only declarations,
  such as narrowing an inherited attribute or Pydantic field type, or
  static-versus-runtime definitions with an `else:` branch; mark its import
  `# noqa: TID251`.
- Code that inspects signatures without needing annotation values should use
  `annotationlib.Format.FORWARDREF`.
- Frameworks that inspect signatures while a class is being created resolve
  lazy names immediately; keep the names in such signatures free of import
  cycles.
{% elif minimum_python.split('.')[1] | int >= 14 %}Annotations are deferred (PEP 649); do not use
`from __future__ import annotations`.

- Put imports needed only for type checking under `if TYPE_CHECKING:`.
- Names in annotations that are evaluated at runtime, such as Pydantic models,
  validated or dispatched functions, and signatures inspected by frameworks,
  must be imported at runtime.
- Code that inspects signatures without needing annotation values should use
  `annotationlib.Format.FORWARDREF`.
{% else %}- Start modules with `from __future__ import annotations`.
- Put imports needed only for type checking under `if TYPE_CHECKING:`.
- Names in annotations that are evaluated at runtime, such as Pydantic models,
  validated or dispatched functions, and signatures inspected by frameworks,
  must be imported at runtime.
{% endif %}
## Design

- Prefer legible, concise, minimal code.
- Avoid unnecessary abstractions and large collections of private helpers.
- Inline very short helpers.
- Do not extract simple logic merely because it appears once or twice. Keep
  such logic at its call sites when it is likely to need independent changes.

## Tests

- Keep test suites DRY and concise.
- Use pytest fixtures, parametrization, and mocks where they make tests more
  readable or remove duplication.
